00:00:23.778 --> 00:00:25.320
The first thing I'm
going to do, just

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.590
to cue up Sandeep's demos,
is give a bit of background

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.470
about why Istio
can be difficult,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:36.750
and what we're really
here today to talk about.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.990
This slide may be
familiar to a lot of you.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.260
When you move from a sort
of monolithic application

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.430
into microservices, a lot
of challenges can arise.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.560
When you start
decoupling your code

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.630
into maybe different
languages and frameworks,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.183
and different
services, it can be

00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:54.600
tough to keep track
of everything.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.203
Things like service discovery.

00:00:56.203 --> 00:00:57.870
All of a sudden you
have lots of network

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.880
calls that you may
not have had before.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.880
This can cause congestion
in the network reliability.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.970
So inevitably, with the
complexity of microservices,

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.010
there's more things
to keep track of

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.800
and things inevitably go down.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.660
Security-- you now have
more points of entry

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.310
into the application.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.640
Instead of one firewall
rule, you might have many.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.720
And finally, aggregating
metrics and logs.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.860
So you need kind
of a way to bring

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.530
all of the information about
your services together.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:30.160
Ideally into something like
a single pane of glass.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.700
And while Kubernetes addresses
some of the scaling and life

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.800
cycling challenges
of microservices,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.010
and is an incredibly
powerful piece of technology,

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.200
it doesn't solve
all those problems.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.000
So the goal of Istio is to help
unify microservices and the way

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.790
that you manage them through
an additional set of platform.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.570
To give a review of
what Istio provides,

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.940
and some of the things
we'll be diving into today,

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.760
it's a somewhat
complex architecture

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.470
in which there are
multiple control plane

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.020
components managing a
set of envoy proxies.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.320
So at the bottom there,
we have the control plane.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.930
Consists of Pilot,
Mixer, Galley, Citadel,

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:14.120
which handle traffic
management, telemetry policy,

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.400
config ingestion, and
certain keys respectively.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.400
We will go into some
of the specifics today.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.980
And then up there at the top,
we have the envoy proxies.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.330
These comprise the data plane.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:29.180
These are the things
that we are configuring

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.070
via the Istio control plane.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:32.300
And what does Istio get you?

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.290
It helps address some of
this complexity challenge.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.900
Unified traffic management,
centralized policy checks,

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.390
telemetry ingestion,
custom adapters

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.200
for the logs and metric
system of your choice,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.410
and automated service identity,
so that with Istio you

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.040
can turn on mutual TLS end
to end service encryption

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.535
with a single policy.

00:02:56.788 --> 00:02:58.270
But--

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.070
Raise your hand
if you have tried

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:04.480
to bring Istio into production
at your organization.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.160
Keep your hand up if it
has been a challenge.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.330
Istio is still a somewhat
new piece of technology.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.300
It's gone through some
changes, released 1.1

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.520
a couple of weeks ago.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.780
And there are some challenges
when adopting Istio, especially

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.820
on top of Kubernetes.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.370
And the first is that, with
those multiple control plane

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.190
components that I showed in
that diagram, there's just a lot

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:30.970
going on.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.460
All of the pieces
kind of fit together

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.530
and generate a lot of logs.

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:40.390
The APIs that Istio exposes
are broad and complex

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.897
and highly configurable.

00:03:41.897 --> 00:03:44.230
There are many, many ways to
configure a virtual service

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.470
in Istio, and this can get
challenging when something goes

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.240
wrong because you're
like, is this even

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.400
a valid virtual
service, for instance.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.400
There's a lot of paths
to a failing state,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.820
and we're here today to
demo some of those paths.

00:04:00.805 --> 00:04:02.180
The other thing
we want to stress

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.310
is that, as developer
relations for Google,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:08.300
we really want to know
how folks are using Istio,

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.690
and where are the gaps in the
tooling and in the ecosystem

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:15.200
so that we can help try to
address that at Google and out

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.519
in the broader community.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:18.920
So we did want to mention that.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.750
You'll see us using things
