00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.410
So we're going to just start
around traffic management.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.520
So how do you debug
Istio traffic routing?

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.980
So as a quick
recap, the way Pilot

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.550
works, you take your virtual
service rules, your gateway

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.510
rules, your policies, you push
them up to the control plane.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.580
Pilot make sure those
rules get pushed out

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.850
to all the envoy proxies.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:08.300
And there are
mechanisms built in

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.750
to figure out, are my envoy
proxies in sync with the pilot

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:13.280
rules that were
sent to the cluster,

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.340
and what are those differences.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.040
And we can see and
examine the configuration

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:16.957
on both sides of that.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:22.680
So the traffic API, and this
is just a small subset of it.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.555
We couldn't really go
through all of it here.

00:05:24.555 --> 00:05:26.220
But just as again a
quick recap, there

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:29.100
is this idea of sort of
north-south, inbound and

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.662
outbound traffic into and
out of your service mesh.

00:05:31.662 --> 00:05:33.120
And then there's
east-west traffic,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.900
which is in and out traffic
within the service mesh itself.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.865
So north and south are Gateway
and Service Entry respectively.

00:05:40.865 --> 00:05:42.990
And then within a cluster,
or within a service mesh

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.390
I should say, the east-west
is Virtual Service

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.710
and Destination Rule.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.170
So those are the objects that
we use to configure things

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:53.370
like traffic splitting, traffic
steering, or ingress or egress.

