Well, what that's telling us is
it's not directing the traffic

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:38.850
to a particular version.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:41.560
It's just sending the traffic
out to weather backend.

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:44.640
And again, we're falling back to
Kubernetes round robin routing.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.640
It's not using the Istio service
mesh routing that we set up.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:50.770
So now it starts a little
bit more interesting.

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:52.562
This is where you're
going to start digging

00:12:52.562 --> 00:12:56.770
into the Istio control proxy
configuration commands.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:59.460
So Istio Control is
a binary that we ship

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.660
with the Istio release itself.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:05.060
And so that actually has a ton
of useful debugging commands.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:08.250
And I'm going to scroll
up a little bit here.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.740
But it's a great, powerful
tool to really dig

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:13.970
into how the proxy is configured
and how things are set up.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:15.720
So here we're actually
going to use things

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.450
like proxy status
and proxy config

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:21.840
to dig into where the problem
is, and try to understand

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:24.075
it a little bit better.

00:13:24.075 --> 00:13:26.700
So the first thing I'm going to
do is check their proxy status.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:31.920
And so we can see that, for all
of our proxies-- and the ones

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.530
we care about are in the
default namespace of weather

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.750
backend multiple,
single, and frontend--

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.077
and those are all synced.

00:13:38.077 --> 00:13:39.660
All those rules are
current and synced

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.180
and they're getting them from
this particular pilot pod.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.960
We can check the weather
frontend one specifically.

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:50.490
You always have to specify the
pod name and the namespace.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.310
And so here what we'll see--

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:00.010
again, the cluster
configurations match,

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:01.940
the listeners match,
and the routes match.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:04.090
So what that's telling
us is that, again,

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.620
the envoy proxy knows
everything that Pilot knows.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:08.260
So the rules we
push the Pilot, they

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:10.540
have made their way over to
the onboard proxy running

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:11.522
in weather frontend.

00:14:11.522 --> 00:14:13.730
But we still haven't figured
out what the problem is.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:15.765
So how do we again
continue to dig further?

00:14:15.765 --> 00:14:17.890
And that's where the proxy
config command comes in.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:25.200
So proxy config on frontend
will show us a little bit more

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:26.087
detail.

00:14:26.087 --> 00:14:27.670
There's a couple of
sub-commands here.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:30.510
So you want to check the
first cluster configuration

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:32.820
on the frontend pod.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.520
And what this will
tell us is all

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:40.140
of the sort of
outbound components

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.450
that it can send things
to are inbound services

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.010
that it can send information to.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:47.610
So again, we see here we've got
weather backend with no version

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.310
information, and it knows
that it can send things

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.845
to multiple and single.

00:14:52.845 --> 00:14:54.720
But the question is,
why is it not happening?

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:56.512
So it looks like, again,
the configurations

00:14:56.512 --> 00:14:57.660
have made their way across.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:59.410
Well let's check one
more component, which

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:01.770
is the outbound routes
from that envoy proxy,

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:04.330
because that could
be another issue.

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:09.430
So again, Istio Control, proxy
config on the frontend pod,

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:11.650
and we're going to
look at the routes.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:17.090
So these routes are
just based on port name.

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.660
And so we see that there are
a bunch of series of routes

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:21.730
just for individual
different services running

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:22.990
inside the service mesh.

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:24.550
In particular, our
weather backend

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.710
is running on port 5,000.

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:28.930
So let's take a look at
that one in depth here.

00:15:35.908 --> 00:15:37.950
All right, so we could
see that one specifically,

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.043
and it's only got
one virtual host.

00:15:40.043 --> 00:15:41.460
So that right there
tells us there

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.770
might be a problem, because we
actually have two hosts there.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:45.145
We've got weather
backend running

00:15:45.145 --> 00:15:47.550
in the V1 and the
V2 configuration,

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:51.610
so we should have more than
one virtual host outbound.

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:53.580
So let's take a
look at that one.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.400
And actually you can
output that as json.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:57.233
Now I'm going to do
this the first time just

00:15:57.233 --> 00:15:59.790
so you can see the output,
because it's lengthy.

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:01.090
This is a lot to go through.

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:04.080
It's lots to scroll up
and try to read through.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.350
So often what I will do in
these scenarios is actually

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:10.350
filter this using a tool called
jq, which just helps you pass

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.930
through json, and I'm actually
going to filter it down

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:16.170
to a particular set of
things I need to look at.

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:25.640
Hopefully that works.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:26.410
All right.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:28.930
So it's showing us all
of the outbound services

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:33.090
it knows about on port 5,000,
and here's the problem.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:36.570
There is actually no route
for this on envoy proxy

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.047
to send traffic to
weather backend.

00:16:39.047 --> 00:16:41.130
It only knows about sending
traffic back to itself

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:44.850
because the front end service
is also running on port 5,000.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:46.170
Well, so what's the problem?

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:47.665
How do we figure out from here?

00:16:47.665 --> 00:16:50.040
Again, we know the rules have
made it all the way across,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:53.310
but there's actually no entry in
the route table for that proxy

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:55.740
to send traffic to
weather backend.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:58.350
Well, as it turns out,
there are two things

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:01.290
you need to do to make
sure your services become

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:03.990
part of the service mesh.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:06.960
The first one is obviously
adding the sidecar proxy.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.710
So you deploy your pod, you
get that sidecar injected

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:13.310
automatically in your pod, and
that makes it part of the mesh.

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:16.327
But there's actually another
sort of esoteric requirement

00:17:16.327 --> 00:17:18.119
that you have to worry
about as well, which

00:17:18.119 --> 00:17:20.777
is in your service definition.

00:17:20.777 --> 00:17:22.319
So remember I've
got weather backend.

00:17:22.319 --> 00:17:23.740
It's got two versions.

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:26.099
So in my deployment I had
two deployments-- weather

00:17:26.099 --> 00:17:28.130
backend single, weather
backend multiple--

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:31.470
and I have one Kubernetes
service fronting them.

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:33.630
In that service
definition, you have

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:38.790
to use a very specific naming
convention for your port.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:41.460
If you just use backend,
it doesn't work.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:43.410
If you come up with a
name it doesn't work.

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:45.330
You actually have to
specify two parts--

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.270
the protocol type,
so in our case http--

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:51.440
and then any kind of
optional identifier.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:53.490
And so we can actually
take a look at the service

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:54.032
real quickly.

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:03.660
And we can see that that
is the problem itself.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:07.950
So here in my service
definition, that's the issue.

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.290
If that requirement
is not met, that pod

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:13.500
doesn't get picked
up inside the mesh.

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:16.860
I'm going to copy and paste this
really quickly from my notes.

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:25.480
So let's go ahead and
apply that updated service.

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.370
And so you can see here what
I did was, all I'm changing

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:31.100
is that one little
port at the bottom.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.750
I changed the name
to http-backend.

00:18:35.524 --> 00:18:38.393
So I'm going to go
ahead and commit that.

00:18:38.393 --> 00:18:40.310
And in a couple of
seconds, you could actually

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:41.768
tail through some
of the envoy logs

00:18:41.768 --> 00:18:43.898
and see new things start
to pop up and happen.

00:18:43.898 --> 00:18:46.190
But we can actually just go
right back to Istio Control

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:48.123
and check that same
command earlier,

00:18:48.123 --> 00:18:49.790
where we checked all
the outbound routes

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:51.665
and we filtered them
down by name and domain.

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:57.250
And now we see that
weather backend

00:18:57.250 --> 00:19:00.670
is a specific available
outbound route.

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:03.940
And if we actually look
at that jq output fully--

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.440
again, I apologize
I'm going to have

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:10.370
to scroll a little bit here just
to show you all this really,

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:11.720
really compelling stuff--

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:15.230
there's actually two entries
in this configuration.

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:19.570
There's an entry for multiple,
which is one of the versions,

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.010
and there's an entry for single.

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:26.368
That means now it knows that on
port 5,000, there's two routes.

00:19:26.368 --> 00:19:27.410
There's one for frontend.

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:28.410
There's one for backend.

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:30.650
And inside that 5,000
route for backend,

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:32.990
there are actually multiple
entries and multiple subsets

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:36.340
it can direct traffic to.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:44.950
Now I'm going to go and
rerun my recipe command just

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:47.110
to see if we get a
difference in version output

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.550
here after 1,000 requests.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:52.050
It's pretty close.

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:54.550
Well, it looks like there was
one little error along the way

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:56.370
there, but we've
got about a 90/10

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:58.392
split between our services.

00:19:58.392 --> 00:20:00.850
So that right there, we're able
to, again, figure this out.

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:03.210
Now what I would say is the
solution to this problem,

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:04.650
turns out, is
relatively trivial.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:06.480
And if you dig through
the documentation,

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:08.580
this requirement is
really well laid out.

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:10.800
Or at least it's
reasonably well laid out,

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.270
in that you have to use the
correct naming convention.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:14.853
But the purpose of
this demo is really

00:20:14.853 --> 00:20:18.090
to show the Istio Control
binary, that mechanism,

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:19.525
is a great debugging tool.

00:20:19.525 --> 00:20:21.900
You still need to have some
other things in your toolbox,

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:25.710
like curl, or even jq or grep
to filter through the output,

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:27.700
because it is quite lengthy.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:29.370
But Istio Control
gives you a lot

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:32.880
of visibility into the different
levels and the different states

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.610
a configuration between, again,
the control plane and the data

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:37.608
plane.

00:20:37.608 --> 00:20:39.650
So we can switch back to
the slides for a second.

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:48.230
So that was the traffic version.

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:49.870
So for the next demo,
what I want to do

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:53.590
is take a quick walk through
on debugging telemetry.

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:57.700
And so again, just a quick
recap on the way Mixer works.

00:20:57.700 --> 00:20:59.410
Those envoy proxy,
that Istio proxy

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:03.050
that sits there inside of your
pod right next your container

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:05.650
is sending telemetry
and metrics information

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:07.990
to Mixer, which is a
control plane component.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:09.550
Mixer then forwards
that information

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:11.930
onto the backend of your choice.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:14.560
In this case it could be
Prometheus, Stackdriver,

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:16.910
Datadog, you name it.

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:19.270
The problem is that
the Mixer configuration

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.290
is hilariously complicated.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:23.600
There's a lot of stuff.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:25.930
So again, our issue
here is that we've

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:30.010
got custom metrics that aren't
showing up in Prometheus.

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:31.830
How do we figure out
what that problem is,

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:33.080
or how do we fix that problem?

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:40.090
So let me just jump over here
back to the demo, please.

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:48.690
And I'm going to switch
to my other cluster.

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:51.192
And this has got a similar
deployment setup already.

00:21:51.192 --> 00:21:53.400
The only difference is I
have a little load generator

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:55.560
script that's running just
to generate some load.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:58.320
So the first thing I want
to do is take a quick look

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:02.500
at Prometheus running
inside this cluster.

00:22:19.818 --> 00:22:21.110
So here's the Prometheus entry.

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:23.485
And I have these metrics in
there that just automatically

00:22:23.485 --> 00:22:26.930
dump out the temperature of the
cities and when it finds them.

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.180
So I can actually start to
look through here and look,

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:30.960
and I'm not seeing
in my metrics.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:33.150
So we know that's
a problem there.

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:36.200
But the quick thing we can do
is check the service discovery

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:39.680
component and see, can
we find our pods in here.

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:43.310
And if you look, we've got 0
to 99 active targets up there.

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:45.650
That means Prometheus
is not picking up

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:47.330
any metrics from our pods.

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:49.400
It's getting the metrics
out of the Istio proxy,

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:54.300
but nothing in particular from
my applications or my services.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:56.340
So we that something's
missing on our end,

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:59.050
because if we actually open
this up and dig around--

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:02.960
I'm just going to show
this relatively quickly.

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:07.440
I think that's probably
somewhat challenging to read.

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:10.080
I'll make it a little bigger.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:12.360
So here's the weather backend.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.190
Here's the weather
backend rule, and we can

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:15.687
see that it's getting dropped.

00:23:15.687 --> 00:23:17.520
It's not picking anything
up because I'm not

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:18.570
publishing anything.

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:21.180
And that's the
solution in this case.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:22.920
I'm actually not
telling Prometheus

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:26.400
how to scrape the metrics out
of my out of my container,

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.210
out of my service
container itself.

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:34.360
So the easiest way to do that
is to add some annotations.

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:39.157
And I've actually got a
canned version of this

00:23:39.157 --> 00:23:40.740
here that I can show
you very quickly.

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:50.950
And so here's the
bit I need add.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:51.910
Right down there.

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:53.410
Inside each of my
deployments I need

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:56.448
to make sure I specify a
particular set of annotations

00:23:56.448 --> 00:23:58.240
so that Prometheus will
scrape the metrics,

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:01.090
and then include them with
the rest of the Istio metrics

00:24:01.090 --> 00:24:05.110
that it's automatically
pulling in by default.

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:13.940
So I can quickly apply
this new configuration,

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:16.850
and then what will happen,
at a matter of a few seconds

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:18.740
or minutes or so, is
that it will start

00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:21.090
to see those metrics come in.

00:24:21.090 --> 00:24:24.470
We can actually take one
more quick look at Prometheus

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:27.970
and see if that's
working, though.

00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:30.720
So I'm going to
refresh this page.

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:35.100
And what you'll see is, inside
of this target, Istio Secure,

00:24:35.100 --> 00:24:38.060
it's actually got two new
services that it's picking up,

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:40.900
which are the two
I just annotated.

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:42.580
And if we go back
to the graph, we

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:45.460
can actually start looking
for city temperatures.

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:48.220
That's the a custom
metric I'm outputting.

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:49.558
We can execute a graph there.

00:24:49.558 --> 00:24:51.100
So it's only got
one so far, but it's

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:52.225
starting to pick it up now.

00:24:55.020 --> 00:24:57.990
The other components of
Mixer, though, there's

00:24:57.990 --> 00:24:59.970
a lot of other individual
kind of configuration

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:02.860
there that you might
need to look at.

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:06.990
So one of the ways you can do
that is using kubectl commands.

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:10.620
And if you actually pull
all of the API resources

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:13.080
and grep with just
istio.io, you can

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:17.970
see there's quite a bit of
custom resource definitions

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:19.170
that Istio supplies.

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:20.700
So digging through
this by default,

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:22.920
or just as a kind
of a debugging step,

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:24.250
is a little bit challenging.

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:27.570
So there's a great set of
docs on the Istio IO page

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:30.510
that I want to mention.

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:32.640
Inside the help FAQ,
there's a great page

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:33.620
called missing metrics.

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:35.370
And what it does is
it literally walks you

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:39.930
through every possible sort
of debug step of Mixer.

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:43.830
Now, the challenge is that a
lot of these Mixer components

00:25:43.830 --> 00:25:45.690
are very tightly coupled.

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:48.078
So if there is a broken
configuration somewhere where

00:25:48.078 --> 00:25:50.370
maybe a rule is not there,
or maybe some of the metrics

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:54.480
aren't there, or maybe it's not
sending information correctly,

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:56.730
one of the easiest steps to
kind of get things back up

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:59.400
and running is to just reapply
that Mixer configuration

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:01.382
and fill in any gaps
that may have occurred.

00:26:01.382 --> 00:26:03.090
So if we could switch
back to the slides,

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:04.380
I'm going to show a
quick example of what

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:05.047
that looks like.

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:11.180
So what you end up doing
for Mixer debugging

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:13.310
is confirming the report calls.

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:16.280
And that's where you connect to
one of the Istio telemetry pods

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:17.630
and take a look at its UI.

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:20.390
You can actually curl
against the current metrics

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:22.400
it's sending out to
Prometheus, and it actually

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:23.900
has a bunch of
diagnostics in there.

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:25.407
So you can check those calls.

00:26:25.407 --> 00:26:27.740
And you could also look at
just individual configuration

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:30.890
problems, or handlers,
or logs themselves.

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.740
For the handlers it just depends
on what your outbound Mixer

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:34.568
config looks like.

00:26:34.568 --> 00:26:36.110
If you configured
it for Stackdriver,

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:38.600
you want to see Stackdriver
handlers and adapters.

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:41.000
If you have Prometheus
and Grafana,

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:42.982
you want to see
Prometheus rules in there.

00:26:42.982 --> 00:26:44.690
If you're looking at
some of the metrics,

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:48.500
you want to make sure that the
metrics for request count, TCP

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:51.240
request size, bytes, et
cetera, those are in there.

00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:53.450
But again, if
something is missing,

00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:56.332
it's very hard to pick out
one component and reapply it.

00:26:56.332 --> 00:26:58.040
So one of the things
I often end up doing

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:01.250
is pulling down the
latest release of Istio.

00:27:01.250 --> 00:27:02.750
In this case, I
used the helm chart.

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:05.270
I generate the helm chart
for the full install

00:27:05.270 --> 00:27:09.907
with Mixer on and Mixer off,
I take the diff of those two,

00:27:09.907 --> 00:27:12.240
and then I take that little
bit that's left over-- well,

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:12.740
not little.

00:27:12.740 --> 00:27:14.150
It's still relatively long--

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:16.460
and then I push that config
back up to their cluster.

00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:17.960
So I'm basically
filling in anything

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:19.168
that might have been missing.

00:27:19.168 --> 00:27:22.160
Any missing metrics
definitions, any missing rules,

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:25.530
that kind of configuration
kick will solve that problem.

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:27.990
It's not a great
solution at the moment,

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:31.160
but for kind of a
weird set of issues,

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:33.440
that might be your best bet.

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:35.732
Now I think if there are
individual like metric handler

00:27:35.732 --> 00:27:37.232
things missing you
might be able to,

00:27:37.232 --> 00:27:39.770
again, extract the little
parts of the configuration.

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:42.590
But because there's so many CRDs
and it's very tightly coupled,

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:44.210
it's hard to pick and choose.

00:27:44.210 --> 00:27:46.040
Sometimes it's easier just
to reapply and make sure it's

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:47.332
filling in whatever is missing.

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:52.630
With that, I'm going to
hand it off back to Megan

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:56.560
to talk about security.

00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:59.510
MEGAN O'KEEFE: Thanks.

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:00.483
Awesome.

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:05.630
I know it's kind of a
whole bunch of demos

00:28:05.630 --> 00:28:12.440
and I'm here to do one last
one all about authentication.

00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:15.640
So I kind of had to choose
one security demo to do.

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:18.040
So Istio supports
both authentication

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:19.160
and authorization.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:23.920
So Istio supports end user
authentication with JWT tokens,

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:27.370
as well as service to service
authentication mTLS, as well

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:28.900
as Kubernetes RBAC.

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:31.180
So I had to kind of
choose one, and I

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:35.550
chose mTLS authentication.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:36.460
How does it work?

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:38.127
I'm going to give a
quick recap before I

00:28:38.127 --> 00:28:40.470
dive into the demo about
what we should expect

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:43.170
to be seeing when
we turn on mTLS,

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:47.873
mutual TLS, authentication,
for one service.

00:28:47.873 --> 00:28:49.790
The first thing that
happens in the data plane

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:51.480
is a TLS handshake.

00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:54.020
So an exchanging of
certain keys from service A

00:28:54.020 --> 00:28:58.520
to service B. Then service A
does a secure naming check,

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:01.070
and what this means is
that Istio has a mechanism

00:29:01.070 --> 00:29:05.240
to ensure that a client
side service can make sure

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:08.060
that the service side service
is authorized to be running it.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:10.760
And what this actually means
is a comparison of the service

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:14.420
account against the actual pod
that's running, making sure

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:16.730
it's the real service
B. And finally, there

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:21.050
is a TLS connection
that is established.

00:29:21.050 --> 00:29:22.970
What is the actual
Istio architecture

00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:24.800
that makes this happen?

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:26.720
So there's two control
plane components

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.220
involved in Istio mTLS.

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:29.600
The first is Citadel.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:31.670
Citadel is responsible
for generating

00:29:31.670 --> 00:29:35.690
the certain keys for each of
the sidecar enabled services.

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:39.080
And what Citadel will do
is generate a cert key pair

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:41.900
for the service
account in Kubernetes.

00:29:41.900 --> 00:29:46.400
So in my case today, it's just
the default service account.

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:48.950
And then when you enable
sidecar injection,

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:52.160
as I have today for my entire
default namespace on my GKE

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:56.350
cluster, when a new
pod is spawned in GKE,

00:29:56.350 --> 00:30:00.140
Kubernetes will mount that cert
key pair, that Citadel created,

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:01.202
as a secret.

00:30:01.202 --> 00:30:02.660
Sorry, it's a secret
in Kubernetes,

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:05.300
and Kubernetes mounts
it into the pod.

00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:07.280
So whether or not
you've enabled mTLS,

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:09.472
the cert key pair is there.

00:30:09.472 --> 00:30:11.180
What this means is
that when you the user

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:15.250
decide that you want to enforce
strict mTLS for a service,

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:19.220
a namespace, or the
entire mesh, you can do so

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:21.810
and it happens very quickly.

00:30:21.810 --> 00:30:25.090
So what you would do as the
user is create a policy,

00:30:25.090 --> 00:30:30.190
an Istio policy API object, add
it to the Kubernetes API server

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:34.190
through kubectl,
and Pilot, in turn,

00:30:34.190 --> 00:30:36.410
is watching the
Kubernetes API server

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:39.240
and creates an envoy filter
and pushes that down.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.260
So it's a multi-step
process, and we're here

00:30:42.260 --> 00:30:47.150
today to talk about when
this thing does not go right.

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:51.680
So in this demo, I will be using
the same sample app, weather

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:57.110
info, and I want to enforce mTLS
for only the weather backend.

00:30:57.110 --> 00:31:01.210
So this is the yaml that you
would apply to make this work.

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:04.430
It's an Istio policy object, and
you can see there at the bottom

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:06.340
it says peers:mtls.

00:31:06.340 --> 00:31:08.430
And I know the syntax
is a bit weird.

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:11.580
It was designed to grow in case
we support more things later.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:15.610
So it's just mtls, open bracket.

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:19.320
So in this demo, I will be
showing the following things.

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:21.630
So for simplicity
purposes, I only

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:24.750
have one version of the backend
that I'm sending traffic to,

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:26.370
the multiple version.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:29.040
And what I want
to see is turning

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:34.080
on mTLS for the backend,
and seeing a nice 200 happen

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:35.950
between frontend and backend.

00:31:35.950 --> 00:31:39.500
But what I'm seeing
instead is a 503.

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:41.550
So let's walk through that demo.

00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:44.180
So if we can switch
to my terminal please.

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:46.160
I know we've had some
glitchy issues that

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:48.260
are sort of Black Mirror
esque on the screen here,

00:31:48.260 --> 00:31:50.480
and I apologize for that.

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:52.490
So here I am in my terminal.

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:53.273
I'm not as brave.

00:31:53.273 --> 00:31:55.440
I'm not going to be typing
out every single command,

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:57.680
so bear with me as
I go into VS code

00:31:57.680 --> 00:32:01.520
and copy paste
some commands here.

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:05.870
Like in the previous demos, I'm
in a cluster with the weather

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:07.040
info app deployed.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:10.120
I already have Istio
installed on this cluster,

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:12.900
but I do not have any
mTLS policies enabled.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:16.690
So if I run kubectl
get policies,

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:18.640
I can see there's
nothing there quite yet.

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:20.570
You can see here in 2 out of 2.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:22.870
What that means is that
