00:32:50.540 --> 00:32:53.242
So if we go into the
Istio documentation

00:32:53.242 --> 00:32:55.450
on authentication-- if you've
ever been to this page,

00:32:55.450 --> 00:33:00.070
it's a bit long but has a
ton of useful information.

00:33:00.070 --> 00:33:01.680
What it says here,
if we scroll down

00:33:01.680 --> 00:33:04.800
to Authentication
Policies, that if you

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:06.960
want to enable client
side authentication

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:11.430
rules with mutual TLS, you need
both a policy and a destination

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:12.210
rule.

00:33:12.210 --> 00:33:14.400
And just straight off the
bat, that can be tricky.

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:16.150
You're like, oh I just
need like a policy,

00:33:16.150 --> 00:33:18.780
just like [INAUDIBLE] TLS,
but what this actually means

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:20.820
is that if you have
a destination rule,

00:33:20.820 --> 00:33:24.440
you might need to actually
change it to enforce mTLS.

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:31.500
So if I go into the policy
I'm about to enforce,

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:33.480
the reason you
need two resources

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:37.060
is because the policy is what
tells the weather backend

00:33:37.060 --> 00:33:40.800
to receive less traffic,
but the destination rule

00:33:40.800 --> 00:33:46.340
is what tells the clients of
the weather backend to use TLS.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:49.607
So mutual means both.

00:33:49.607 --> 00:33:51.690
So what we're going to do
is apply these resources

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:55.395
to the cluster and
hope for the best.

00:33:55.395 --> 00:33:57.520
And I know yesterday I used
a lot of shell aliases,

00:33:57.520 --> 00:33:58.937
so I'm not going
