that maybe something

00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:58.580
was weird with the certs and
keys is kind of not true.

00:37:58.580 --> 00:38:01.250
That part seems to be OK.

00:38:01.250 --> 00:38:03.770
So now what?

00:38:03.770 --> 00:38:08.900
You might be wondering,
what else would envoy

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:11.940
be able to report back to
us that would be helpful.

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:14.430
And what I'm about to
do might sound crazy,

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:17.150
but I'm actually going
to increase the log level

00:38:17.150 --> 00:38:20.720
and on void to debug
from default info

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:24.560
to see if we can say anything
more about why requests

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:26.790
to the back end are failing.

00:38:26.790 --> 00:38:29.300
So in order to do that--

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:31.670
so when you have
an envoy container,

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:33.590
it actually exposes
an admin console.

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:34.480
It's actually a UI.

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:35.720
I'm not going to show
it today, but there's

00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:37.280
some helpful stuff in there.

00:38:37.280 --> 00:38:40.920
And you can send requests
to that admin console

00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:44.540
to do things with that very
specific envoy by proxy.

00:38:44.540 --> 00:38:48.100
So what I'm going
to show here is

00:38:48.100 --> 00:38:50.740
calling that admin console
to enable trace level

00:38:50.740 --> 00:38:53.390
logs for only the backend.

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:55.595
So you can see here, across
all of envoys loggers,

00:38:55.595 --> 00:38:57.220
I've just upped the
log level to trace.

00:39:00.290 --> 00:39:02.530
And so what we hopefully
will start to see here

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:06.550
is, if I refresh this
page a whole bunch more,

00:39:06.550 --> 00:39:11.078
which is interesting,
there's a whole bunch of logs

00:39:11.078 --> 00:39:11.870
that are coming in.

00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:14.230
You can see it's now like
debug level, and it's like, OK

00:39:14.230 --> 00:39:15.960
how do I even parse this?

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:17.980
And there are a lot
of ways to do it.

00:39:17.980 --> 00:39:19.780
Just for purposes
of this demo, I'm

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:23.140
going to do a grep because
I ran this demo before

00:39:23.140 --> 00:39:25.330
and I know what I'm looking for.

00:39:25.330 --> 00:39:28.670
And to do a grep on
the word handshake.

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:31.255
So if something went wrong
with the exchanging of certs

00:39:31.255 --> 00:39:38.310
and keys during that
initial DLS handshake error,

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:41.100
we might be seeing a
TLS handshake error.

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:52.682
So you'll notice that in
the last couple of seconds,

00:39:52.682 --> 00:39:54.140
we got a whole
bunch of these debug

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:57.560
logs from envoy that are saying
external envoy SSL handshake

00:39:57.560 --> 00:39:59.030
error one.

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:01.460
So what this would tell
me, if I enable mTLS,

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:04.970
is that the very first step of
that TLS connection happening,

00:40:04.970 --> 00:40:06.597
that very first step failed.

00:40:06.597 --> 00:40:08.180
And I know I have
valid certs and keys

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:11.570
from the envoy proxies,
so I that can't be it.

00:40:11.570 --> 00:40:16.220
So typically at this point
I start to blame myself.

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:18.140
What did I do wrong
on the config side

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:21.150
to make this
horribleness happen?

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:25.250
So what I will do is go into all
of the resources that I added

00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:26.780
and try to rack my brain.

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:28.172
Oh gosh, what went wrong?

00:40:28.172 --> 00:40:29.630
So remember, at
the very beginning,

00:40:29.630 --> 00:40:32.480
to make all of this break,
we added a policy one

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:35.700
and a destination rule too.

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:39.850
What is responsible for
each of these components--

00:40:39.850 --> 00:40:42.690
so policies for the server side,
destination rule for the client

00:40:42.690 --> 00:40:43.410
side--

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:45.570
I will now use
the Istio CTL tool

00:40:45.570 --> 00:40:48.180
to try to figure out if
there is a conflict going on

00:40:48.180 --> 00:40:49.890
in my configuration.

00:40:49.890 --> 00:40:52.830
So Sandeep showed the Istio CTL
command for traffic routing.

00:40:52.830 --> 00:40:55.500
I'm going to show
the same command line

00:40:55.500 --> 00:40:57.960
tool but just a
different command, which

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:00.600
is authentication TLS check.

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:03.540
And what this does is
compares the destination rule

00:41:03.540 --> 00:41:08.100
and the policy for one service
to see if there is a conflict.

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:10.862
And let me get this
a little bit better.

00:41:10.862 --> 00:41:12.570
So what we see here
for whether backend--

00:41:12.570 --> 00:41:15.600
so I called this command for
just the weather back end--

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:19.380
what we see here is status
conflict, server mTLS,

00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:21.780
client HTTP.

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:23.310
That's not what mTLS is, right?

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:26.830
What we hope to see is
server mTLS, client mTLS.

00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:28.930
So what's happening here?

00:41:28.930 --> 00:41:33.000
What this tells me is that if
we go back to these resources,

00:41:33.000 --> 00:41:35.770
destination rule is what tells
clients of the weather backend

00:41:35.770 --> 00:41:37.000
to use TLS.

00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:38.395
This is the problem.

00:41:38.395 --> 00:41:39.770
This is where I
am at this point.

00:41:39.770 --> 00:41:42.320
I'm like, OK, there's something
up with my destination rule.

00:41:42.320 --> 00:41:43.300
What do I do?

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:45.520
So at this point, what
I will do is run kubectl

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:51.400
get destination rule and see, oh
gosh no, I have two destination

00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:54.380
rules for the same service.

00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:55.670
This is not good in Istio.

00:41:55.670 --> 00:41:57.650
You do not want overlapping
destination rules

00:41:57.650 --> 00:41:59.340
that say different things.

00:41:59.340 --> 00:42:01.460
So what I'll do at
this point is see

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:02.840
what's actually going on there.

00:42:02.840 --> 00:42:04.610
So I'm getting those
two destination rules

00:42:04.610 --> 00:42:07.040
and I'm just going to show
the whole yaml output.

00:42:09.790 --> 00:42:12.120
So we see both of the
destination rules here.

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:13.470
This is the one I added.

00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:17.350
So DR whether backend is the one
I added just a few moments ago.

00:42:17.350 --> 00:42:18.610
And this one is good.

00:42:18.610 --> 00:42:20.220
Enforce Istio mutual.

00:42:20.220 --> 00:42:21.450
This is what I expect to see.

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:23.610
I scroll down, I see
the second destination

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:27.120
rule that was added by
somebody, probably me an hour

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:30.360
ago, that said,
actually, traffic policy

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:33.180
TLS mode disable.

00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:35.460
So at this point, envoy
is very confused in

00:42:35.460 --> 00:42:37.800
that it thinks it needs
to be doing TLS and not

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:39.400
TLS at the same time.

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:41.583
503.

00:42:41.583 --> 00:42:43.500
That's kind of an
oversimplification of what's

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:44.917
actually happening
under the hood,

00:42:44.917 --> 00:42:46.560
but what we would
do to fix this is

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:57.940
we would delete the destination
rule that we added before,

00:42:57.940 --> 00:43:00.940
and we would modify
the destination rule

00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:02.650
we already had.

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:05.620
Surprise, it was in here.

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:14.010
This one that said
TLS mode disable.

00:43:14.010 --> 00:43:16.720
We would modify that
to enable mTLS on it.

00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:18.520
So I have a file that
does exactly that.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:19.740
It's here.

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:21.240
I'm going to apply
it to my cluster.

00:43:26.650 --> 00:43:28.410
And what we hope to
see, crossing fingers,

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:31.480
is that we can get
back to the backend.

00:43:31.480 --> 00:43:34.230
So this doesn't show every
single piece of the debugging

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:35.790
mTLS, but I hope
it kind of sheds

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:38.370
some light about
how not to enter

00:43:38.370 --> 00:43:40.080
a complete and
total state of panic

00:43:40.080 --> 00:43:42.520
when things go
wrong on that end.

00:43:42.520 --> 00:43:46.490
So if we can go back
to the slides, please.

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:47.470
Thank you.

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:51.950
That was just the backup video.

00:43:51.950 --> 00:43:54.630
All right, so let's
wrap up a little bit.

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:57.090
What did we learn in this talk?

00:43:57.090 --> 00:43:58.650
We only had a certain
amount of time,

00:43:58.650 --> 00:44:00.775
so we did kind of have to
pick and choose the demos

00:44:00.775 --> 00:44:02.150
that we did.

00:44:02.150 --> 00:44:04.040
But things that we
looked at is like, how

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:06.540
to determine if a virtual
service is actually working.

00:44:06.540 --> 00:44:08.480
How to use Istio
CTL and the envoy

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:14.150
logs and other tools to really
dig into what's happening.

00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:16.280
Sandeep showed us
how to diagnose

00:44:16.280 --> 00:44:19.070
missing metrics in Prometheus.

00:44:19.070 --> 00:44:25.180
And finally, I just showed
transport level authentication.

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:28.010
Here is a short and
honestly abridged

00:44:28.010 --> 00:44:31.340
list of some of the stuff
we used in this talk.

00:44:31.340 --> 00:44:33.260
This is not every
single tool that you

00:44:33.260 --> 00:44:35.000
can use to diagnose Istio.

00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:36.950
We know that the tools
ecosystem is lacking,

00:44:36.950 --> 00:44:38.950
and we are talking about
it, and it is something

00:44:38.950 --> 00:44:41.210
that we are extremely aware of.

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:43.430
In a perfect world,
I never use curl

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:45.240
to diagnose when
Istio is going wrong.

00:44:45.240 --> 00:44:47.180
We need something a
little bit more scalable.

00:44:47.180 --> 00:44:49.105
If the promise of
Istio is automation

00:44:49.105 --> 00:44:50.480
and being able to
manage hundreds

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:52.487
or thousands of
microservices, we

00:44:52.487 --> 00:44:53.820
need something better than curl.

00:44:53.820 --> 00:44:56.810
So I'm super excited for
the coming months and years,

00:44:56.810 --> 00:45:01.090
and the growing
ecosystem that is Istio.

00:45:01.090 --> 00:45:01.990
This is not the end.

00:45:01.990 --> 00:45:03.907
If you are still here
for the rest of the day,

00:45:03.907 --> 00:45:09.010
there are multiple other
Istio talks, including VM mesh

00:45:09.010 --> 00:45:11.890
expansion, CICD
stuff with Istio,

00:45:11.890 --> 00:45:14.320
as well as a discussion
of Traffic Director

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:17.073
if that's of interest to you.

00:45:17.073 --> 00:45:19.240
A quick call out that we
are conducting user studies

00:45:19.240 --> 00:45:21.520
in the coming months for Istio.

00:45:21.520 --> 00:45:23.440
If you would like to
join these user studies,

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:26.330
you can grab that QR
code or go to that link.

00:45:26.330 --> 00:45:28.150
Our UX team would
love your feedback

00:45:28.150 --> 00:45:30.200
and your participation.

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:31.120
Thank you so much.

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:34.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

